  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a “dual passivation” strategy for stabilizing a lithium metal interface with a polymer coating. It depicts lithium metal at the bottom, a polymer layer in the middle, and electrolyte above. On the left, the coating appears to contain fluorinated aromatic units that help form LiF at the lithium surface, suggesting one protective passivation pathway at the metal side. On the right of that same panel, an additive labeled DFOB is shown moving into the coating region, while PF6 species in the electrolyte are blocked from entering, indicating a second passivation function at the electrolyte side: selective interfacial chemistry and suppression of unwanted salt-derived reactions.\n\nThe companion panel presents the intended coating design in terms of four coupled properties. For transport, the coating should allow Li+ movement while limiting electron flow, as suggested by opposite dashed pathways. For kinetics, a circular arrow near the interface highlights interfacial charge-transfer behavior, labeled with exchange current density. Under reactions, the coating seems to regulate decomposition or interphase-forming events at the boundary. Under mechanics, arrows labeled with modulus and surface energy-like terms indicate that the layer should also provide mechanical robustness and control interfacial stress or wetting. Overall, the figure conveys a multifunctional coating that chemically and physically stabilizes lithium metal.",
